Since April, people have told us their bookings land on the wrong day: a month flipped. The
desk merged every report of it into this one ticket and called it known. It wasn't one issue.

<Callout kind="warn">
Split into two tickets: the shared parser's month-first read
(<a href="https://github.com/titusandronicu/mega-dev/issues/6">desk ticket #6</a>, 379 reports)
and <code>booking-v3</code>'s own date hint reading the opposite way
(<a href="https://github.com/titusandronicu/mega-dev/issues/7">desk ticket #7</a>, 33 reports).
Full breakdown and how we found the second one: <a href="/kb/incidents/4471-split">#4471, split</a>.
</Callout>

## What was merged

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The <code>hint</code> row above still reads "not this ticket — no ticket of its own" — that's
`ticketCause()` naming which cause owns *this* ticket's number, not whether a ticket exists for
it. It does now: desk ticket #7. 5 of the parser reports were read by the desk as a one-day
move rather than a month/day swap (the note below the table, from `desk-notes.json` via
`readAs()`); they're still parser reports, just footnoted — not a third cause, see
[#4471, split](/kb/incidents/4471-split) for why.

## Since April

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

## Lessons

- Merging by subject (decision 0001) finds reports that are the same wording; it doesn't check
  they're the same bug. Two different bugs can produce the same complaint ("wrong day") and
  land in one ticket. Merge by subject to triage fast, then check cause before calling it one
  issue.
- Dedupe by `id` before you count anything — the export reused 15 ids across pages. "412
  reports" was never typed here again after that was found; the table above computes it.
- Write the page while the queue is still in your head, and come back to it when the real
  breakdown changes — a known ticket that's actually two tickets is a stale page, not a closed
  one.

